#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "f042812b-fb14-520a-bb6d-08631d941d04" :question/math-academy-id "e-5586" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d29d0f5dd5e0d2c79c8d2c40c49b358a01dc5bb5536153fe05068773ef1e228.svg)\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $\\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of $q_{11} \\cdot r_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ![](images/71/714ad80d47ea58159e0e486f5bfc5a6657fd441bc47aa2b8fa7230a89bdb0445.svg)\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{12}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/a6/a6377dfb1e7ed7afafe348354dd21b2195c12495d9a5a6dcd98fec3aaeb8162d.svg)\n\nFinally, $q_{11} \\cdot r_{12} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right) \\cdot \\left({2\\sqrt6}\\right) = 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133633 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "c6fe3751-c00b-592b-903e-567d95174cd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5959" :question/problem "![](images/db/db19bfb8029513dcb3dc109a0752f87ecf405a6aa56881849f553d30fa1085ff.svg)\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\big\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_1\\Vert =2,$ $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_2\\Vert =3,$ $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_3\\Vert =4,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-3{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $r_{13}+r_{23}+r_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=3{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2,\\mathbf{q}_3 \\big\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f5/f53a5eb3b2d24ad186382f8eed42775b2ee61d34a20a0af821a38c17d846e873.svg)\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n![](images/27/2797cd9e375b3a28f3f8afc8c49b85e202ad5cc87fb5a332ebb9abd73f388ee3.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nr_{13}+r_{23}+r_{33} = 6+(-6)+4 = 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133678 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "0a812585-d467-562d-acda-e379ac9dc544" :question/math-academy-id "e-8324" :question/problem "![](images/db/db0726bd1986ffe1876c4b5a93b7b14b4f4c1496dc6b28ebd6ac002ea878cde2.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by $Q^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that $Q^TQ = I_2$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133679 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/id #uuid "155ed7f9-909e-5550-b1ef-f707b49f7fde" :question/math-academy-id "e-8325" :question/problem "![](images/12/128c3de38ca9c80e3e0d22d939fe05c88404fed97dee1a94f59aff5331f56172.svg)\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns $\\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of $q_{12} \\cdot r_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct a matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2\\mathbf{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by $Q^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/5d/5d8b0271c6dcd970ec7c8d652433a798daf97e67792c47db1f6eef4bd39ad651.svg)\n\nFinally, $q_{12} \\cdot r_{22} = -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\cdot 3 = -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133680 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/id #uuid "35fdd570-1c80-5b0d-9c5f-fa277bd30afb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8326" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix\n\nII. The columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\nIII. $R$ is an upper triangular matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{A}\n=\n\\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{Q} \\times \\underset{{\\large n} \\times {\\large n}}{R},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n \\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be a $3\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be a $2 \\times 2$ upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd89ee68-349c-5c55-bfb4-0640100d85d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451317} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0d0530-fb2a-55ee-8c99-4b946a8d080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f0d0530-fb2a-55ee-8c99-4b946a8d080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451318} :answer-field/id #uuid "32ce223a-7bf9-5b24-9d75-5a1ee1be8cee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452506} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80aff2bb-c529-532f-bd61-5a4a757274f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451319} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8b817d-950d-56d2-a00f-a66cc027dd62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80aff2bb-c529-532f-bd61-5a4a757274f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451319} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea9e0400-ff35-582c-afa0-5b642cb65d38" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452507} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20a8fdb-8267-5e02-9526-345d5877f1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451321} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bdf3d-57db-5827-9f8f-4a457f333e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bdf3d-57db-5827-9f8f-4a457f333e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451322} :answer-field/id #uuid "46443e21-8812-5903-b11e-26709577b9c0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ea55d3c-3433-59ef-97d4-45fcd98ab0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-140896" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $5\\times 4$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The columns of $Q$ are orthogonal\n\n- **II.** $Q$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix\n\n- **III.** $R$ is a  square  matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are orthogonal.\n\n- Statement II is false. $Q$ should be a $5\\times 4$  matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be a $4\\times 4$ upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef9dba6-3f12-5da9-b1c0-57877064fcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451323} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccbf279-9ed0-556d-9395-eaabb82722b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ef9dba6-3f12-5da9-b1c0-57877064fcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451323} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e589c4-8feb-50f2-94e5-91dc5bf78939" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47423d03-c26d-53a6-97f0-da0ec40e24ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451325} {:answer/id #uuid "59929f29-b3e2-5f68-ad2f-45acb0eee989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59929f29-b3e2-5f68-ad2f-45acb0eee989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451326} :answer-field/id #uuid "947adca6-648c-56d1-8cd2-dda98433a243" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d589cba1-1f3b-5854-9859-9dd1c62ec2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451327} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c8f22c-d237-5016-ab0c-213ce6e6e57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d589cba1-1f3b-5854-9859-9dd1c62ec2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451327} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee67563d-5792-5feb-9fa1-bed677e18320" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1442b18-9e38-5f57-94c8-12000a66d6da" :question/math-academy-id "q-140897" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Q$ is an $n\\times m$  matrix\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)$ is spanned by the columns of $Q$\n- **III.** $R$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A),$ they span $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\n- Statement III is false. $R$ must be an $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f77c16-15f2-5876-91ea-1f42c2f49146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432273} {:answer/id #uuid "040cb4ad-6adb-5b45-a356-8c24b8b87c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432274} {:answer/id #uuid "589b7138-e564-53cf-b058-e2dd1efd855b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432290} {:answer/id #uuid "6d46eee3-4e02-5798-955c-810b16c0b207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432297} {:answer/id #uuid "e88e24de-26c6-5e79-8da5-47c1a175c8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "040cb4ad-6adb-5b45-a356-8c24b8b87c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432274} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed11c1c1-5852-58e2-a3f1-e49b2bb10b4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5f7e59e-edf4-549f-aea0-27ed8989142e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140901" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{1}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &={v}_{1}+{v}_{2}={q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=-{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=-{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=1+(-1)+3=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a027607c-b49e-5d51-8e9f-696efe340642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363226} {:answer/id #uuid "66dfc0c2-4c41-5b5c-8ca9-8963c9494355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363227} {:answer/id #uuid "92170d95-d6d3-5882-96d9-9f06a90067ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363228} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8b6e55-0d08-5b04-9194-57e762fe0902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363229} {:answer/id #uuid "25a800e8-0c9b-5cbf-9949-6d4a63eb0a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66dfc0c2-4c41-5b5c-8ca9-8963c9494355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363227} :answer-field/id #uuid "48f82e91-6709-5adc-99bd-29df5cbf4521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "836a6cda-4c5e-5dc1-9726-f5d184348fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140902" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f9e469-7eec-5401-8986-a4974df572b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363233} {:answer/id #uuid "bbffa08b-7606-549f-bc87-1f500316ebd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363234} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ab06c1-b10b-5935-bf2d-75749c31b94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363235} {:answer/id #uuid "85bd57ed-0fc2-5736-8094-14f443d99ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363236} {:answer/id #uuid "c5eaeb1b-839d-5f69-b76b-0cdf9c511c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85bd57ed-0fc2-5736-8094-14f443d99ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363236} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8043045-7145-51b3-822c-f2d744e6043c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95a58504-d1c2-5301-9dd6-5bea44e00bd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140903" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35450620-e1c1-5553-8d89-babffec49fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388794} {:answer/id #uuid "9509bf92-21de-58cf-a928-febc07388708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388795} {:answer/id #uuid "eba19a2d-d967-5a67-9c11-60ad3d49f2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388796} {:answer/id #uuid "ab55f8c6-2adf-5f61-aac6-7bf5271da29d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388797} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe3e312-3a15-5ca5-b692-01ea036db64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe3e312-3a15-5ca5-b692-01ea036db64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388798} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf69525-3508-5fe7-99c0-fbb77383cf15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fb99b60-4344-5bc1-af82-6f45b3524b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-140911" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(\\frac{4}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbcea61a-f7c5-5e90-95bf-e30e97fb1c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392397} {:answer/id #uuid "87a540ed-333f-527d-82f8-aa109a53d564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392398} {:answer/id #uuid "10193a27-2949-56aa-bfb7-aac130ca8727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392399} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce7f102-17d8-5df4-8505-ab11eafbcb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392400} {:answer/id #uuid "5f83a4ce-78d8-5078-bb57-e6fcb33b7384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10193a27-2949-56aa-bfb7-aac130ca8727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392399} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac78dc7d-8434-5df2-894a-caad12e5602a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77f48d85-1f2c-59c8-b53a-85db3bc9d5a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-40838" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{12}{6})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{6})=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5efe4aae-2768-528f-a3cc-4c9b9a907391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379830} {:answer/id #uuid "a5705d08-f2bc-5a4f-b1ab-65641a5b0ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379831} {:answer/id #uuid "bf62a0e5-63e3-5e42-889b-986f2738de40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379832} {:answer/id #uuid "e87b5762-481c-57bd-ba1b-c18fef4d4c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379833} {:answer/id #uuid "411c482c-f2e8-5cb9-b82c-84752b240dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5705d08-f2bc-5a4f-b1ab-65641a5b0ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379831} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20ad309-608a-5c10-aca7-0636d5c13dac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fc92fc5-cd9a-5882-b3a5-3c9b7ba00cb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-41417" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=4,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=6,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{6}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=8{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=4{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=16{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+16+6=26.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1020e81-b421-586a-8b8a-f4f50d0b8d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132170} {:answer/id #uuid "c019a536-7f70-5b76-b99c-efb116b36d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132171} {:answer/id #uuid "334623f1-ef55-5d7f-a31e-9d1ff64b56a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 2\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132172} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7aa6a9-7ba0-514f-869d-3dc935626d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{10} & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132173} {:answer/id #uuid "773a1618-24ee-5c22-971b-ad3632d4aa3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132174}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf5785c-f9f4-59f9-99c3-49639109b1e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132169} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e0be982-1b80-5738-8cd6-74f57650e028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392410} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba57cc6-4b0a-5d92-8761-d49b07038502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 2\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392411} {:answer/id #uuid "bce0ce7a-b8f8-5ff0-8fc3-71570e52bd82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392412} {:answer/id #uuid "34f1627d-9591-5047-a498-be9818dce031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{10} & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392413} {:answer/id #uuid "0346b6b2-402b-56a4-86b2-20a38185f353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e0be982-1b80-5738-8cd6-74f57650e028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392410} :answer-field/id #uuid "013bd0a2-7475-538a-af66-e5aa29d3dd0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a949ac3-27ed-549f-9f9c-cfbce0083312" :question/math-academy-id "q-41418" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ will give a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74849d77-3f75-57fb-9eda-c7eb146ef089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364180} {:answer/id #uuid "ae17bbad-6406-590f-9037-515c2d2e3302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364181} {:answer/id #uuid "80193887-56ef-5f55-9899-81a4ca2a5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364182} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ed3821-6db7-500c-afa6-af890beb1af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364183} {:answer/id #uuid "ee368a24-e35e-5530-bc4c-c0b11699a84f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80193887-56ef-5f55-9899-81a4ca2a5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364182} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfd9129a-1c55-5b4e-92c4-8f9a2d89199f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0711fb88-caa5-5f45-81df-dbacaf0d183c" :question/math-academy-id "q-41486" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+8{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}-3{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+8{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}-3{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-16{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=9{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -16 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}=9+2+3=14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be78f97c-a223-5ab6-b494-bf9064deb166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133315} {:answer/id #uuid "076084fc-5240-5f13-bc41-f4207752e1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133316} {:answer/id #uuid "1dcf723b-8f97-5504-88a1-7f4a31a1e4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133317} {:answer/id #uuid "32bf15c7-9ae6-5051-b497-8d314f8c95a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133318} {:answer/id #uuid "41151fbb-e388-5e0f-828b-a2e497a4ca4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be78f97c-a223-5ab6-b494-bf9064deb166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133315} :answer-field/id #uuid "af4ec3ba-fc26-5e37-bc3f-2f09063c1d7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b75151e1-73fe-588a-be1a-8c3a00e71cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-95165" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $4\\times 3$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The columns of $Q$ are orthogonal\n\nII. $Q$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix\n\nIII. $R$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are orthogonal.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q$ must be a $4\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. $R$ should be a $3\\times 3$ upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0346398f-65b8-5bd3-b0f9-8b628f52a353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452221} {:answer/id #uuid "bceb1ecc-febc-5631-a278-5aed524c28fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bceb1ecc-febc-5631-a278-5aed524c28fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452222} :answer-field/id #uuid "97cbf390-2de4-5d0c-9c7a-49c1601d1072" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452958} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3b4bd9b-5b77-560c-a577-20bdb4c3f47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452223} {:answer/id #uuid "dedc586f-9e2a-5d88-add5-529a718afa93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dedc586f-9e2a-5d88-add5-529a718afa93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452224} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78d5e63-ee82-56c8-9657-4763f39441dd" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452959} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c92a537c-38be-543f-9b04-f1d82d249904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452225} {:answer/id #uuid "e48f546a-1da8-5e18-b960-859e9f202044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e48f546a-1da8-5e18-b960-859e9f202044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452226} :answer-field/id #uuid "120438b3-dec2-597e-a996-fd9ef95e4b98" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14a18e20-8a94-54a8-afff-0a503b7d967c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95916" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The columns of $Q$ are orthonormal\n\n- **II.** $Q$ is a square matrix\n\n- **III.** $R$ is a square matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are orthonormal.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $Q$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix, it is square.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $R$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix, it is square.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fafecd-7387-5634-828c-573f1744befa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454214} {:answer/id #uuid "19a442fc-481d-58d8-9541-569ca4faf6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67fafecd-7387-5634-828c-573f1744befa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454214} :answer-field/id #uuid "5edb2284-a520-5008-b822-43d1ea474475" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454857} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58652c7b-c953-5c9f-809b-80c39c943d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454216} {:answer/id #uuid "4713a280-f45c-5164-a1e2-ba8421f47c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4713a280-f45c-5164-a1e2-ba8421f47c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454217} :answer-field/id #uuid "6346abcd-d6c5-5edc-add2-dd9c1ca312a0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454858} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63f0bb0f-b477-52fc-b037-337d27feba6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454218} {:answer/id #uuid "2a679340-6bec-5785-8c26-90181ea28dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f0bb0f-b477-52fc-b037-337d27feba6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454218} :answer-field/id #uuid "ded79da7-007a-52a0-869d-714c07ace673" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b19eecb-3ecc-5584-a617-4bf8675e0b01" :question/math-academy-id "q-95917" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $4\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Q$ is a $4\\times 2$ upper triangular matrix\n\n- **II.** The columns of $Q$ are unit vectors\n\n- **III.** $R$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be a $4\\times 2$ matrix, but is not necessarily upper triangular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are unit vectors.\n\n- Statement III is false. $R$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd1aa0c-d1f1-5e19-8c7c-5cd02697146c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432291} {:answer/id #uuid "6759f5b6-d249-5365-af63-b9e06fadd6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432295} {:answer/id #uuid "8130fec1-ce90-56ae-8304-b2a9297e4bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432300} {:answer/id #uuid "965ab4c3-b74e-52d8-a355-600cbb8426c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432302} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e8a3d2-47ca-598e-8298-401f66b07744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6759f5b6-d249-5365-af63-b9e06fadd6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432295} :answer-field/id #uuid "34f73934-6b3e-5362-85fb-c078d25b34ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1307433-5f5d-5677-8396-c15cc88708bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95924" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f156e046-fa0d-5803-9d38-472b3668c9cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364587} {:answer/id #uuid "c218b0b0-ec5c-5f5e-b8ce-248a499d3534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364588} {:answer/id #uuid "69ec7d58-d9c0-5c9d-88fd-f9271de5e19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364589} {:answer/id #uuid "df9b7366-5dbb-5188-8dca-12fd2020f705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364590} {:answer/id #uuid "84264b94-1397-5e4e-a6e5-70dde4b0df3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df9b7366-5dbb-5188-8dca-12fd2020f705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364590} :answer-field/id #uuid "68de1a49-3945-5406-b9a9-df87423f479c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abcc772f-aef1-5bb8-8a2c-19affc4a7f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95931" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6541fae-ca98-5011-918c-a2e113b5f4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364601} {:answer/id #uuid "bf8150ed-2bbb-58ff-9b44-a56ebd9adcf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364602} {:answer/id #uuid "c064a103-378c-5690-860f-e149d7fc799b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364603} {:answer/id #uuid "4915b708-42a8-572b-b6a9-51e3c5b54b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364604} {:answer/id #uuid "ef65c06a-43c4-5f72-8a34-4f1944f04e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6541fae-ca98-5011-918c-a2e113b5f4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364601} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4def03-8d7b-5557-86f1-b0596397f5f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3eed5db2-4721-55a5-93e3-3a839fa38279" :question/math-academy-id "q-95940" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=3,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=2,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-3{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{1}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=3{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=2{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set ${{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-3{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=3{q}_{1}+3{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}={q}_{1}+6{q}_{2}+2{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=3+3+6=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4900a2e7-4ec1-5045-a690-39e44e6c099b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432286} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed122b1-a8ef-54bc-a477-491273558134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432288} {:answer/id #uuid "4edcb67b-0b50-5e1a-880f-c692e26158f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432289} {:answer/id #uuid "988c19f4-4b4b-5658-b6a9-5508c8ebffd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432303} {:answer/id #uuid "a409649e-ea5b-5f5d-997d-61959f000b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "988c19f4-4b4b-5658-b6a9-5508c8ebffd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432303} :answer-field/id #uuid "039c8e12-c757-5b90-a48b-67f394056d16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "946d71c0-c7c2-59d9-aaa7-5ecb48201289" :question/math-academy-id "q-96015" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f4dfc0-0618-5204-93e9-55df6839e9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415486} {:answer/id #uuid "320cae01-cb6d-546b-8fae-e515a79d9a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415489} {:answer/id #uuid "4258535d-b0d0-5712-835e-79ab5aee8c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415490} {:answer/id #uuid "7422fe19-732e-5581-b615-9ad03e55d8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415492} {:answer/id #uuid "d0567542-26c9-51bb-b549-cf91536f2136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7422fe19-732e-5581-b615-9ad03e55d8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415492} :answer-field/id #uuid "84ec1a22-6484-5de6-851b-ba1cc03d10d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b80e943-ecec-54be-8ddd-ec70be544570" :question/math-academy-id "q-96027" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} & \\frac{4}{9} & \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f8fc6e-2098-5281-b2be-91340c5382ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364615} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9e3bfa-4b1c-5d94-adef-a29f1d34c643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364616} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bad7d9-6fa9-551b-bbe8-3c2e9b0daab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364617} {:answer/id #uuid "2247ba90-0af6-5144-9a63-c4d44090d3ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364618} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f7efcb-48ba-5cb1-951a-6612de87d27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bad7d9-6fa9-551b-bbe8-3c2e9b0daab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364617} :answer-field/id #uuid "96755057-b7af-57c3-90a7-4ebdd3f1b964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75e8d7ef-26a5-51d9-ba2c-c43a53de12e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96029" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e36d9c-e9a2-5446-ad66-2c509425fb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364622} {:answer/id #uuid "6838046c-6bb6-5382-aff4-79052bb83389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364623} {:answer/id #uuid "66729efb-cb03-5e0d-975f-24a9597e8440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364624} {:answer/id #uuid "0e78a432-6e13-5220-8bcd-195f0ec965d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364625} {:answer/id #uuid "eac12345-022f-5cbe-aa4a-767d1ed83136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66729efb-cb03-5e0d-975f-24a9597e8440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364624} :answer-field/id #uuid "736f5b90-2f10-5a15-8216-cf8f1586fc1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c7952d1-1f1c-50a2-a342-5271912d43de" :question/math-academy-id "q-96033" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{6}{18})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3\\sqrt{2})=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "991358c9-3100-5464-96bd-f2c50332af98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510008} {:answer/id #uuid "9d04ddc7-88d1-5961-b149-0ed80dedcd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510016} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c797d4-4e44-5a31-83c8-b4dceca7a07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510098} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd3705e-81e5-5864-b1ab-6314a9b9569b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510117} {:answer/id #uuid "f86da36a-fea9-5185-a415-54e83a58db6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "991358c9-3100-5464-96bd-f2c50332af98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510008} :answer-field/id #uuid "31551476-3b52-57ca-b4de-8bda2f7a95d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ccedda5-a7d6-563d-a624-831df95d1f38" :question/math-academy-id "q-96039" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{24}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (4\\sqrt{3})=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072eda89-f2fd-5e6b-8865-e919b2deb069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509707} {:answer/id #uuid "6acfb0da-27b6-5749-b6e8-62595ebc8056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509908} {:answer/id #uuid "7cce974d-ff76-5e44-b28b-aec2865278ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509957} {:answer/id #uuid "9557cf45-32ca-5990-b9e7-8944ea966b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510002} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b55a48-54cb-5cad-b04e-d24581a88559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9557cf45-32ca-5990-b9e7-8944ea966b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510002} :answer-field/id #uuid "36df5534-c619-54f6-902b-ac3c15f3e762" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca2de93c-f55b-512e-9e27-ab6ff5bd3cb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96041" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=2.$"}]}
